dict(num=3, collection="A", mode="Scaffold", title="The Last 10% Protocol",
lead="A defined closing ritual for any deliverable. <b>The hardest part of finishing becomes a checklist, not a judgment call</b>, run in one pass before anything ships.",
drain="For WHY-WHAT and WHAT-WHY profiles the finish line moves: the interesting problem is solved, energy has already shifted to the next thing, and the closing details, quality passes, edge cases, handoffs, feel like the work is over. That last ten percent is where reputations quietly leak.",
does="The protocol turns closing into a fixed sequence of five passes, run in order, every time. Nothing relies on noticing what needs finishing; the ritual asks the same questions of every deliverable. It takes thirty focused minutes and converts almost-done work into actually-done work.",
device_title="The five closing passes",
device=[("Quality pass","Read or run the whole thing once, end to end, as the recipient will."),
("Edge cases","What breaks at the boundaries, the first use, the odd input, the handoff?"),
("Handoffs","Who receives this, and do they have everything they need to act on it?"),
("Loose ends","Open comments, placeholder text, unsent notifications, stale links."),
("Sign-off","Named person confirms done against the definition of done.")],
steps=[("Schedule the pass as its own block.","Thirty minutes, separate from the making. Closing is a different mode of work."),
("Run the passes in order.","The sequence matters; quality before edge cases, edge cases before handoffs."),
("Use the checklist physically.","Print it or keep it open. The point is to remove judgment from the process."),
("Log anything you skip.","A skipped pass is a decision. Write it down so it is a choice, not a leak.")],
wont="It will not make finishing feel satisfying the way starting does, and it cannot replace deep review on complex technical work. It guarantees the floor: nothing ships without the same five questions being asked of it.",
kit=["Closing pass checklist","Printable desk card","Skip log"],
pairs=[(2,"Pre-Flight Checklist Library"),(7,"Definition-of-Done Cards"),(4,"The Detail Debt Log")]),

dict(num=4, collection="A", mode="Support", title="The Detail Debt Log",
lead="A running capture list for details consciously deferred in the moment, reviewed on a fixed weekly rhythm. <b>Deferring stops turning into dropping.</b>",
drain="In the flow of purpose and progress work, small details surface constantly: a number to verify, a person to loop in, a setting to change. Handling each one in the moment breaks momentum; ignoring them means they vanish. Tertiary HOW profiles lose these details not through carelessness but because nothing catches them.",
does="The log is the catch. Any detail you choose not to handle now goes on the list in one line, with enough context to act on later. A fixed weekly review clears the list in a single batch, at a time you chose, instead of details resurfacing as surprises at the worst moments.",
device_title="What a log entry captures",
device=[("The detail","One line, specific enough that future-you knows exactly what it means."),
("Where it lives","The doc, thread, or system it belongs to."),
("What breaks if dropped","Honest stakes. Some details can genuinely be let go."),
("Clear-by date","When it stops being deferrable.")],
steps=[("Keep the log one gesture away.","A pinned note, a card in your tracker. Friction kills capture."),
("Capture instead of handling.","In the moment, one line on the log. Momentum stays intact."),
("Clear it weekly, in one batch.","Thirty minutes, same time each week. Batch-clearing details costs far less than scattered handling."),
("Prune honestly.","If something has rolled three weeks and nothing broke, cross it off on purpose.")],
wont="The log does not do the details, and the weekly clearing session will still be draining work; schedule it at a high-energy time and keep it short. What it removes is the second, worse cost: details lost entirely and discovered by someone else.",
kit=["Log template (print + digital)","Weekly review ritual card","Prune criteria"],
pairs=[(5,"Timeboxed Detail Sprints"),(3,"The Last 10% Protocol"),(31,"Energy Budget Planner")]),

dict(num=5, collection="A", mode="Support", title="Timeboxed Detail Sprints",
lead="A method for batching detail work into short, scheduled, high-energy blocks. <b>The drain gets contained to windows you chose</b>, instead of leaking across the whole week.",
drain="Detail work resisted is detail work that spreads. Left unscheduled, tertiary HOW tasks get pushed to the end of tired days, done badly, or avoided until they become urgent. The drain is real either way; unmanaged, it also comes with guilt and worse output.",
does="Sprints flip the ownership. Similar detail tasks get batched and assigned to short blocks, twenty-five to forty minutes, placed at your highest-energy point in the day, with a hard stop and recovery time in primary-orientation work immediately after. Same work, dramatically lower cost per unit.",
device_title="The sprint recipe",
device=[("Batch","Group similar detail tasks; context-switching multiplies the drain."),
("Place","Schedule at peak energy, never at the end of a depleted day."),
("Box","Twenty-five to forty minutes with a visible timer and a hard stop."),
("Recover","Follow immediately with work in your primary orientation. Recovery is part of the method.")],
steps=[("Collect detail work into a single list.","The Detail Debt Log feeds this directly."),
("Book two sprints a week, recurring.","Protected calendar blocks at your best hours. Treat them like client meetings."),
("One category per sprint.","Numbers sprint, formatting sprint, follow-up sprint. Batching is the multiplier."),
("Stop at the timer.","Unfinished items roll to the next sprint. The hard stop is what makes the commitment sustainable.")],
wont="Sprints reduce the cost of detail work; they do not turn it into something you will look forward to. If sprint volume keeps growing week over week, that is a structural signal: the role is carrying too much tertiary load, and the Triage Decision Tree is the next stop.",
kit=["Sprint planner sheet","Batching categories card","Calendar block templates"],
pairs=[(4,"The Detail Debt Log"),(32,"Tertiary Triage Decision Tree"),(31,"Energy Budget Planner")]),

dict(num=6, collection="A", mode="Scaffold", title="The Edge-Case Question Bank",
lead="A printed set of boundary-testing questions to run against any plan. <b>You borrow the signature question of the HOW orientation as a tool</b>, without needing the instinct behind it.",
drain="HOW-primaries generate edge cases naturally: what happens when volume doubles, when the key person leaves, when the input is malformed. For WHY-WHAT and WHAT-WHY profiles those questions do not arrive on their own, and plans ship with untested boundaries that fail in production, in front of clients.",
does="The bank supplies the questions. Fifteen minutes running a plan against the list surfaces most of what a precision review would catch at the structural level. You do not have to imagine the failure modes; you only have to answer prepared questions honestly.",
device_title="Questions from the bank",
device=["What happens when the volume doubles? When it halves?","Who owns this after launch, and do they know?","What is the first thing that breaks under time pressure?","What are we assuming stays constant that might not?","What happens at the boundaries, the first day, the last day, the handoff?","If the key person is out for two weeks, what stops?"],
steps=[("Run the bank before commitment.","The questions are cheapest before the plan is locked and public."),
("Answer in writing, one line each.","\"Fine\" is not an answer. If the honest answer is \"don't know,\" that is a finding."),
("Flag the top three exposures.","Not everything needs fixing. Name the three that would hurt most."),
("Assign a counter to each flag.","An owner and an action per exposure, added to the plan before it ships.")],
wont="A question bank is not a substitute for domain expertise; it finds structural gaps, not technical ones. For systems with real complexity, use the bank first, then bring the flagged items to a HOW-primary, you will have a far better conversation.",
kit=["Question bank card deck","Exposure log sheet","Facilitation guide for team use"],
pairs=[(30,"Pre-Mortem Protocol"),(10,"HOW-Partner Review Protocol"),(27,"Assumption Audit")]),

dict(num=7, collection="A", mode="Scaffold", title="Definition-of-Done Cards",
lead="A card completed at the start of any piece of work that specifies, objectively, what finished means. <b>Done becomes a fact, not a feeling.</b>",
drain="Without an external definition, \"done\" for a tertiary HOW profile is a feeling, and the feeling arrives early, usually when the interesting part is over. The gap between felt-done and actually-done is where rework, resentment from receivers, and quality surprises live.",
does="The card externalizes the judgment. Before work starts, it captures the objective criteria, the person who confirms them, and, just as important, what is explicitly not included. Finishing becomes checking a card, which requires no precision instinct, only the discipline to write the card first.",
device_title="Anatomy of a card",
device=[("The deliverable","What is being produced, in one plain sentence."),
("The criteria","Three to five checkable facts that must be true. No adjectives."),
("The confirmer","The named person who says done. Not you."),
("The not-included line","What this work explicitly does not cover, agreed up front.")],
steps=[("Write the card before starting.","Five minutes at kickoff. After work begins, the definition bends toward the feeling."),
("Make every criterion checkable.","\"Reviewed by legal\" is checkable. \"High quality\" is not."),
("Agree it with the receiver.","The card is a contract. Their signature criteria beat your assumptions."),
("Check the card, then stop.","When criteria are met, ship. The card also protects you from polishing past done.")],
wont="A card cannot rescue vague work; if you cannot write checkable criteria, the work itself is not yet defined, which is a finding in its own right. And meeting the card is the floor, not the ceiling; judgment still matters above it.",
kit=["Blank card pads (print)","Example cards for common deliverables","Criteria writing guide"],
pairs=[(3,"The Last 10% Protocol"),(11,"Good-Enough Threshold"),(2,"Pre-Flight Checklist Library")]),

dict(num=8, collection="A", mode="Scaffold", title="The Effort Reality-Check",
lead="A structured correction for the chronic underestimation of execution effort. <b>The estimate gets built from tasks and history</b>, not from optimism.",
drain="Estimation lives in the HOW orientation: it requires seeing all the tasks, including the invisible ones. Tertiary HOW profiles estimate from the shape of the work, which reliably misses reviews, revisions, coordination, and cleanup, and the shortfall lands as missed deadlines and weekend recoveries.",
does="The worksheet forces the estimate through a sequence that surfaces the invisible work. Every task gets listed and sized, the standard hidden categories get added explicitly, and the total gets compared against a real past project before a multiplier is applied. The output is a number you can defend.",
device_title="The correction sequence",
device=[("List every task","Use the decomposition worksheet. Estimates on unlisted work are fiction."),
("Size each one","Small, half-day, full-day. Rough is fine; complete is mandatory."),
("Add the invisible work","Reviews, revisions, approvals, coordination, cleanup. It is never zero."),
("Compare to history","Find one comparable past project. What did it actually take?"),
("Apply the multiplier","Whatever ratio history shows, apply it. The pattern is more honest than the plan.")],
steps=[("Never estimate from the shape.","If the tasks are not listed, the estimate is not real yet."),
("Do the history comparison out loud.","Saying \"the last one took double\" makes the multiplier undeniable."),
("Present the corrected number first.","Anchor on reality; let others argue it down explicitly if they choose to."),
("Log actuals when it ends.","Every completed project sharpens your personal multiplier.")],
wont="This corrects structural underestimation; it cannot price genuinely novel work where no history exists. In those cases the honest output is a range with named unknowns, and the worksheet will at least make the unknowns explicit.",
kit=["Reality-check worksheet","Invisible work checklist","Actuals log"],
pairs=[(1,"Vision-to-Task Decomposition"),(15,"Milestone Backplanning"),(10,"HOW-Partner Review Protocol")]),

